content.text:
Good ask, and the "based on flexible pricing" part is the crux — it changes the number a lot, and not in the direction people expect.

## The metric

Self-consumption saving = PV that covered your own load instead of being imported, valued at the **gross retail price in force at that moment**:

```dart
/// Money not spent because own PV covered load that would otherwise have been
/// imported: per bucket, `pv − export`, valued at the gross price in force then.
///
/// Priced per bucket, never at a window average, deliberately: PV peaks at
/// midday when the spot price is at its lowest, so averaging systematically
/// overstates what self-consumption was worth under a dynamic tariff.
///
/// A counterfactual, not a cash flow — unlike [exportRevenueCents].
double? selfConsumptionSavingCents(EnergyHistoryData? history) {
  if (history == null || history.points.isEmpty || points.isEmpty) return null;
  final bucketHours = history.bucket.inSeconds / 3600.0;
  var cents = 0.0;
  var any = false;
  for (final p in history.points) {
    final price = currentAt(p.time);       // hour-resolution gross price
    if (price == null) continue;           // only where the curve covers
    final selfW = p.pvW - p.gridExportW;   // generated minus what left the house
    if (selfW <= 0) continue;
    cents += selfW * bucketHours / 1000.0 * price.ctPerKwh;
    any = true;
  }
  return any ? cents : null;
}
```

It mirrors `importCostCents` exactly — same per-bucket loop, same "only where priced" rule, same units.

**Three things worth knowing about the result:**

1. **It will be much larger than feed-in.** Savings are valued at the *gross* consumer price, which `EnergyPrices.points` already carries (`fromProto` applies markup + VAT — ~26 ct/kWh with your tariff), whereas feed-in is flat ~7 ct. Same kWh, roughly 4× the value. That contrast is the actual insight.

2. **But flexible pricing cuts it down** versus a flat-tariff intuition. Your solar peaks around midday, which is exactly when today's curve bottoms out — the controller's current curve runs from ~1.6 ct at 11:00 up to 24 ct in the evening. So midday self-consumption is worth a fraction of evening self-consumption, and only the per-bucket calculation shows that honestly. A 24 h average would inflate it.

3. **The figure is inherently sound**, which is a nice property: since `consumptionW = pv + import − export`, we have `selfW = pv − export ≤ consumptionW` always, so it can never claim you saved more than you consumed. No clamping needed.

**One limitation to state in the UI or accept silently:** battery round-trips are excluded. `EnergyHistoryPoint` carries only `pvW/gridImportW/gridExportW/loadW` — battery exists solely as a window total (`batteryChargeKwh`/`batteryDischargeKwh`), so discharge can't be priced by time. Irrelevant for your current setup (grid meter + solar inverter, no battery), but it would need a per-bucket battery series from the controller to be right later.

